<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awa Province (Tokushima)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awa was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the east of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture">Tokushima Prefecture</a>. In the 16th century it was the home base of the Miyoshi clan, retainers of the Hosokawa who rose to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. It was overrun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chosokabe Motochika</a> in the 1580s and then given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> to the Hachisuka family, who ruled it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a> domain until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima.jpg" alt="Awa Province (Tokushima)" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Awa_(Tokushima).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Awa was bordered by the provinces of Tosa, Sanuki and Iyo and was one of the provinces of the Nankaidō circuit. Under the Engishiki classification it was one of the 35 superior countries in importance and one of the middle countries in distance from the capital. Its provincial capital stood in what is now the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a>, and its short name was Ashū. People have lived there since the Japanese Paleolithic, and settlements and burial mounds of the Yayoi and Kofun periods have been found, above all in the fertile Yoshino River valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the Kogo Shūi the name was first written with the character for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, which the province produced; in 713 Empress Genmei changed its spelling when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsuryo">Ritsuryō</a> codes standardised provincial names to two characters. The Sasaki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogasawara-clan">Ogasawara clans</a> were its shugo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period and the Hosokawa in the Muromachi period, until the Miyoshi supplanted them and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</a>, came to rule much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinai">Kinai</a> region.</p><p>When internal conflict weakened the Miyoshi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a> of Tosa invaded and by 1585 had united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Hashiba Hideyoshi's armies defeated him within two months and forced him back to Tosa. Awa went to Hideyoshi's general Hachisuka Masakatsu, who handed it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hachisuka-iemasa">Hachisuka Iemasa</a> because of his age. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> the Hachisuka ruled Awa and neighbouring Awaji from Tokushima Castle; the domain's nominal assessment of 257,000 koku made it the 17th largest. An early Meiji survey counted 584 villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the abolition of the domains in 1871 the province passed through Myōdō Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a> and Kōchi Prefecture before it was separated in 1880 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture">Tokushima Prefecture</a>, whose area it covers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Awa Province (Tokushima)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He overran <b>Awa</b>, Sanuki and Iyo and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The Miyoshi were retainers of the Hosokawa deputy shoguns, with a base in <b>Awa</b> on Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/miyoshi-clan-domain">Miyoshi clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After Toyotomi Hideyoshi conquered Shikoku in 1585, he granted <b>Awa</b> province to his retainer Hachisuka Iemasa, who built Tokushima castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokushima-domain">Tokushima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
